from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path
g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def B(*a): return L(*a,language='bash')

C('git','Git keeps a history of changes to files. It helps you compare versions and work on ideas without losing the story of how a project grew.','Use Git in a new practice folder containing only your own sample files. These commands run in a local terminal, not this website. No online account is needed for these lessons.','https://git-scm.com/docs/gittutorial',[
B('Meet local version history','Version control records changes over time. Git can work on your own computer without a hosting service.','git --version','The command reports the installed Git version. If Git is missing, use its official installation guide with a trusted adult.','Explain the difference between saving a file and recording a version in Git.','Git and GitHub are different tools: local Git does not require a GitHub account.','Does local Git require a GitHub account?','No','Yes'),
B('Start a practice repository','A repository is a project whose history Git tracks. Initialise only a new practice directory for this lesson.','mkdir learning-git\ncd learning-git\ngit init','Git creates its hidden .git directory inside learning-git. That directory holds the repository information.','Run git status inside the new folder.','Do not initialise or change an unrelated system folder.','What does git init create here?','A local repository','An online account'),
B('Inspect the working tree','The working tree is the set of files you edit. status tells you what is new, changed or ready for a commit.','printf "My first note\n" > note.txt\ngit status','Git reports note.txt as untracked because it has not yet been added. The printf command is for a POSIX-style shell such as Git Bash.','Read note.txt and compare the status before and after creating another practice file.','Untracked files are not included in a commit unless you add them.','What does untracked mean?','Git has not added the file to its tracked history','The file cannot be opened'),
B('Stage the intended changes','The staging area holds the changes you intend to put in the next commit. Add only the files you mean to include.','git add note.txt\ngit diff --cached','The staged difference shows note.txt ready for the next commit. Reviewing it helps avoid accidental additions.','Change the file again and compare staged and unstaged differences.','git add takes the current file content; later edits need another add if they belong in the commit.','Which command reviews staged changes?','git diff --cached','git --version'),
B('Make a local commit','A commit records the staged snapshot with a message. Git needs a name and email label, which are part of the commit metadata.','git config user.name "Practice Learner"\ngit config user.email "learner@example.test"\ngit commit -m "Add first practice note"','The local repository records the staged note. These labels are for this practice repository, not a login.','Write a message describing one clear change in your own words.','Real published commits expose their author metadata; do not use personal details carelessly.','What does a commit record?','The staged snapshot','Every unsaved editor tab'),
B('Read a difference','A diff shows how current content differs from another version. Review it before saving the next version.','printf "A second line\n" >> note.txt\ngit diff','The difference shows the added second line. Lines with a plus are additions in the displayed comparison.','Stage the change and compare git diff with git diff --cached.','An empty unstaged diff does not prove there are no staged changes.','What does a plus line usually show in a diff?','Added content','A deleted file only'),
B('Read the history','The log lists commits. The short view helps you see their order and messages without opening each snapshot.','git log --oneline\ngit show --stat HEAD','The log shows the saved commits. HEAD names the current checked-out commit in this normal branch workflow.','Find the message for your first commit.','Commit IDs depend on repository content and metadata; your IDs will differ from another learner\'s.','What does git log show?','Commit history','Only untracked files'),
B('Try an idea on a branch','A branch is a movable name pointing to a line of commits. It lets you work on a change without immediately adding it to another branch.','git status\ngit switch -c club-note','Git creates and switches to club-note. Start from a clean working tree so unrelated edits do not confuse the exercise.','Run git branch to see which branch has a star.','A new branch is not a separate full copy of all files on disk.','What does switch -c do?','Create and switch to a branch','Upload a release'),
B('Understand merging','A merge joins histories. If two branches change the same lines differently, Git may need a person to choose the result.','git log --oneline --all --graph\n# Read the graph before deciding what to merge.','The graph helps you see branch history. Merging is a decision about changes, not a way to hide conflicts.','Draw two branches that edit different lines and explain why their merge may be simple.','Review and commit or safely save your work before changing branches or merging.','Who decides the intended text in a real conflict?','A person reviewing the changes','The file name alone'),
B('Ignore generated files','A .gitignore file lists patterns Git should ignore when files are not already tracked. It is useful for temporary output.','printf "scratch.log\n" > .gitignore\nprintf "temporary\n" > scratch.log\ngit status --short','The untracked scratch.log is ignored. The .gitignore file itself can be tracked.','Add a second temporary file pattern.','Ignoring a file does not remove it from earlier commits or erase a leaked secret.','Does .gitignore erase old committed secrets?','No','Yes'),
B('Inspect remotes','A remote is a named address for another repository. Viewing remotes does not upload or download commits.','git remote -v','A new local repository may show no remotes. This is normal and does not stop local commits.','Explain what the common name origin refers to.','Do not paste secret tokens into remote URLs.','What is a remote?','A named repository address','A required paid account'),
L('Share a clear change','A good shared change has a purpose, a reviewed difference and a short explanation of checks. A hosting service can review it separately from local Git.','Purpose: add club meeting time\nReview: only the intended note changed\nCheck: read the note and verify the time\nMessage: Add club meeting time','The change is easy for another person to understand. Publishing it is a separate deliberate action.','Write a summary for a one-file correction.','Do not upload private files or secrets while practising collaboration.','What makes a shared change easier to review?','A clear purpose and focused difference','Many unrelated edits'),
])

C('linux','Linux is an operating system used on many computers and servers. A shell reads typed commands. We will practise safe commands in a folder made only for these lessons.','Use a Linux terminal or a suitable practice environment. Commands shown here do not run inside this frontend website. Work only with your own practice files; no administrator commands are needed.','https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html',[
B('Find your place','A terminal displays a shell, which reads commands. pwd prints the directory you are currently using.','pwd','The output is the path of your current directory. It differs between computers.','Write down your current path before changing folders.','A terminal command acts in its current directory unless you give another path.','What does pwd show?','The current directory path','The password'),
B('List files','ls lists directory entries. The -a option includes names beginning with a dot, which are often hidden in normal views.','ls\nls -a','The second list can show hidden names such as .git. Neither command changes files.','Compare the two lists in a practice directory.','A hidden file is not automatically private or secure.','Which option includes dot files?','-a','--delete'),
B('Create and enter a folder','mkdir creates a directory. cd changes the shell\'s current directory. Use a new name that does not replace existing work.','mkdir linux-practice\ncd linux-practice\npwd','The final path ends in linux-practice. This is the workspace for later examples.','Create a notes directory inside it.','A relative path starts from the current directory.','Which command moves into a folder?','cd','ls'),
B('Write and read a small file','printf writes text. A redirection sign sends that text into a file. cat prints a file\'s contents.','printf "Moon\nOcean\n" > books.txt\ncat books.txt','The file contains two lines: Moon and Ocean. The single > creates or replaces the named file.','Use a different new file name and write three lines.','A single > overwrites existing contents. Use it only on a practice file you intend to replace.','What does > do here?','Write output into the file','Read the file without changing it'),
B('Append a line','Two redirection signs append text to the end of a file. Earlier content stays in place.','printf "Zoo\n" >> books.txt\ncat books.txt','Zoo appears after the existing lines.','Append a fourth book title.','Do not confuse >> with >, which replaces the file contents.','Which redirection appends?','>>','>'),
B('Copy a practice file','cp makes another copy of a file. Give a new destination name when you want to preserve both versions.','cp books.txt books-copy.txt\ncat books-copy.txt','The copy has the same text at the moment cp ran. Later edits to one file do not automatically update the other.','Append a line to the copy and compare both files.','cp may replace an existing destination; choose a fresh practice name.','Do later edits automatically update both copies?','No','Yes'),
B('Search lines','grep prints lines that match a pattern. With -F it treats the pattern as fixed text rather than a special pattern language.','grep -F "Moon" books.txt','Matching lines containing Moon are printed. This does not edit the file.','Search for a title that is absent.','A search is normally case-sensitive unless an option changes that rule.','Does grep edit the matching lines?','No','Yes'),
B('Count and sort','wc counts things such as lines. sort writes lines in order to its output. Piping connects one command\'s output to another.','sort books.txt | wc -l','The result counts the lines produced by sort. The original books.txt remains unchanged.','Run sort alone to see the sorted titles.','Sorting output does not rewrite the original file unless you explicitly write a result.','What does the pipe connect?','One command\'s output to another\'s input','Two passwords'),
B('Quote paths with spaces','Quotes keep several words together as one shell argument. This matters when a path contains spaces.','mkdir "reading notes"\nls -d "reading notes"','The command lists one directory whose name contains a space.','Create another new directory with a space in its name.','Without quotes, the shell may treat a path as several arguments.','Why quote reading notes?','To treat it as one path','To hide it from every user'),
B('Inspect permissions','Permissions describe who may read, write or execute a file. ls -l shows them along with other details.','ls -l books.txt','The permission letters appear at the start of the listing. Read r as read, w as write and x as execute.','Identify the owner permission group in your listing.','Do not grant every user write access just to make an error disappear.','What does r mean?','Read permission','Rename automatically'),
B('See running processes','A process is a running program. ps shows information about selected processes without stopping them.','ps -f','The output lists processes in the current selection, with IDs and commands. The exact list depends on the system.','Find the shell process in the listing.','Do not stop a process you do not understand, especially on a shared computer.','What is a process?','A running program','A saved image only'),
B('Write a tiny shell script','A script saves commands so you can run them again. This one only prints a greeting and the current path.','printf \'#!/bin/sh\nprintf "Hello, learner\\n"\npwd\n\' > hello.sh\nsh hello.sh','The script prints a greeting and the current directory. Using sh reads the script without needing executable permission.','Change the greeting in this practice script.','Read a script before running it; a file extension does not make it safe.','Why save commands in a script?','To repeat a known set of steps','To avoid understanding the commands'),
])

